**Status: no recommendation yet. Joel picks.**

From [M5](/lore/m5-touch-the-skiff) on, the arm works on a Eurorack skiff: it turns knobs, presses buttons and patches cables.

## What the skiff must fit

- **The Eurorack format.** Panels are 128.5 mm high, and widths are multiples of 1 HP, 5.08 mm ([Doepfer's A-100 construction details](https://www.doepfer.de/a100_man/a100m_e.htm)).
- **The shared base plate.** The arm and the skiff sit on one rigid plate, so a registration stays valid ([Mounting](https://docs.source-robotics.com/PAROL-docs/1.1/04-installation/mounting/)).
- **Reach.** The modules the arm plays must sit inside the arm's workspace ([Kinematics](https://docs.source-robotics.com/PAROL-docs/1.1/03-technical-data/kinematics/)).

**needs_source:** the skiff, its modules and their measured knob and jack positions.

## Terms

- **skiff**: a shallow, often portable Eurorack case. Here, the case the arm plays.
- **Eurorack**: the modular synthesizer format that Doepfer's A-100 defined, with 3U panels on an HP grid ([A-100 construction details](https://www.doepfer.de/a100_man/a100m_e.htm)).
- **HP**: horizontal pitch, the Eurorack width unit. 1 HP is 5.08 mm ([A-100 construction details](https://www.doepfer.de/a100_man/a100m_e.htm)).
